  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1214 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nDrier air tries to push in from the southwest during today as \nthe low finally starts to lift northeast. We are still expecting \nshowers and storms during the afternoon and evening. These \nstorms will be capable of producing brief moderate rain, \nfrequent lightning, small hail, and gusty winds. Colder air in \nthe wake of the front and lingering moisture will allow for \nlight mountain rain and snow showers to linger Sunday night into\nMonday. Again, little snow accumulation is expected, but passes\ncould once again be slippery Monday morning, especially along \nthe Central and Northern Divide. \n\nThis low finally moves east of the Divide by Monday morning, with \nmuch drier conditions expected through the day Monday. A quick \nmoving clipper will just brush our northern border Monday night into \nTuesday, with the potential for some light rain and snow showers \nover the higher terrain of the eastern Uintas and Northern Colorado \nDivide. Behind this clipper, zonal flow takes control, bringing \nmostly quiet conditions and gradually warming temperatures through \nthe back half of the work week. Highs start out 5-10 degrees below \nnormal Sunday through Tuesday, climbing to near-normal by the \nWednesday-Thursday time period. By Friday, flow aloft shifts to a \nmoister southwesterly direction in response to a low deepening off the \nWest Coast, bringing increased cloud cover and a slight cooldown. \nModel agreement is poor beyond this point, but there are indications \nthat we could see a return to cooler and unsettled conditions by \nnext weekend.",
